// @run
// @log 7
// @log 1
// @hint redundant type annotation: `sh` is inferred as `{r: i32[]}[]`
// THE SHADOW TEST, PINNED AT THE SPELLING WHERE IT DECIDES SOMETHING — the local-shadow twin
// of `pin-global-binding-both-scopes.vl`'s leg 5.
//
// D703 makes `monoGlobalLetOf` answer from INSIDE a function, where it used to decline on
// `fnIx >= 0` outright. The decline is now the shadow test itself: a parameter of that name,
// or a `let`/`const` of it anywhere in the body. This file is the second half — an
// un-annotated LOCAL `sh` over a module-scope `const sh: {r: i32[]}[]`, a spelling
// `monoAnnPinName` DOES claim, so if the global scan ran ahead of the shadow test the
// instance would declare a `(ref $rlWrap)` parameter over an i32 caller.
//
// IT USED TO BE A LOUD REJECT and the reject was NOT what the pin owns. The local arm above
// declines, the shadow test keeps the global out, and the cascade then reached
// `exprStruct`/`structIndexOfExpr`, which resolved the IDENT through the MODULE TABLE and
// answered about the global — D705, closed 2026-09-03 by `globalLetOfSidIn`. With every
// classifier's Ident arm asking the frame first, the shadowed name is an i32 all the way
// down and the program runs. What the pin still owns is unchanged: if the shadow test
// regressed, `monoAnnPinName` would take the GLOBAL's annotation and the instance's `x`
// would be a `(ref $rlWrap)` over an i32 caller — a different outcome than `7`.
function idg<T>(x: T): T { return x }
const sh: {r: i32[]}[] = [{ r: [1] }]
function leg() {
  const sh = 7
  print(idg(sh))
}
leg()
print(sh.length)
